/.idea
ravynOS.xcodeproj
/build.log
/build
.DS_Store
/.vscode
/Kernel/xnu/BUILD/
/Kernel/xnu/DEVELOPMENT_X86_64/
/Kernel/xnu/cscope.genhdrs/
/Kernel/xnu/.mach_kernel.timestamp
/Kernel/xnu/.symbolset.timestamp
/Kernel/xnu/xnuVersion
/memory/
/*.cpo.json
/Kernel/xnu/*.cpo.json
/Kernel/xnu/iokit/Kernel/*.cpo.json

# Build products, not source. Libraries/dyld/dyld/dyld is a linked dyld built
# from this tree; it was checked in by mistake and carries absolute host paths
# (/Users/max/Projects/build/...) in its debug strings. It is ours, not Apple's,
# but it is a build artifact and must never be committed. The Apple-derived
# payload that genuinely must not be published is policed separately, in
# tools/bootlab/assets/.gitignore.
#
# NOTE: deliberately no blanket *.o / *.d rule. 1363 .o files are tracked in
# this repository, including unit-test fixtures under
# Libraries/dyld/unit-tests/test-cases/, so a global object-file ignore would
# hide real fixtures.
/Libraries/dyld/dyld/dyld

# The bootlab asset tree. Every file under it except this directory's own
# .gitignore is an Apple product binary, a third-party EFI binary, or a
# regenerable build artifact of this project. Measured 2026-09-28: 45 Apple
# product binaries totalling 11,626,528 bytes. The detailed, per-file
# provenance record is in tools/bootlab/assets/.gitignore, which IS tracked.
#
# The files stay on disk. boot.efi in particular is load-bearing: the boot
# path is host OVMF -> UEFI Shell -> the typed command
# fs0:\System\Library\CoreServices\boot.efi. Ignoring a path does not remove
# it from the working tree, so the lab keeps working while nothing is
# committed.
/tools/bootlab/assets/*
!/tools/bootlab/assets/.gitignore

# bootlab staging output and the per-experiment manifests that select a probe
# as PID 1. Both are regenerable: staged/ is produced by link-static.sh and the
# manifests by make_shell_manifest.py. They are build products, and the
# manifests in particular are one per diagnostic run, so tracking them would
# add noise without adding provenance -- the findings they selected are
# recorded in tools/bootlab/BOOT-PLAN.md instead.
/tools/bootlab/staged/
/tools/bootlab/manifest_*.json

# ...except manifest_cpmv.json, which is NOT a per-experiment manifest. It is
# the durable clean closure the runners gate on (PROVENANCE-PLAN P0): the only
# committed manifest whose closure and provenance both pass. Ignoring it would
# make the gate depend on a file the OS may delete, which is the failure P0
# exists to remove.
!/tools/bootlab/manifest_cpmv.json

# ...and manifest_gui.json, the GUI boot image manifest produced by
# make_gui_manifest.py. It is not a per-experiment manifest either: it stages
# the real WindowServer/Dock/Filer bundles and their runtime closure. It was
# held out of the tree only while make_gui_manifest.py wrote absolute build
# paths for libutil/libicucore.A/libicudata (and later libsqlite3 and
# LaunchServices); those now resolve to Developer/ravynOS.sdk and the bundles
# stage from work/, so every "file" entry is repo-relative and the manifest is
# portable.
!/tools/bootlab/manifest_gui.json

# bmake -j dependency sidecars. The .o files they name are build products; the
# sidecars are the by-product of the parallel make that produced them.
.depend.*.o
*.o.tmp

# In-tree build products from the BSD bmake builds of Libsystem and dyld.
# These trees are built in place (no OBJROOT), so the .o files land beside
# their sources. Scoped to the two build trees on purpose: the repo already
# documents that 1363 tracked .o files exist as unit-test fixtures under
# Libraries/dyld/unit-tests/, and a blanket *.o rule would hide new fixtures.
/Libraries/Libsystem/*.o
/Libraries/Libsystem/**/*.o
/Libraries/dyld/**/*.o

# In-tree build products from the BSD bmake builds. Scoped to the two trees
# that are built in place for the ravynOS closure, for the same reason as the
# rules above: the repo tracks real .o files as unit-test fixtures elsewhere.
/BSD/lib/libedit/*.o
/BSD/lib/libedit/*.a
/BSD/lib/libedit/*.dylib
# libedit's per-source headers, generated by the mkheader rules at
# BSD/lib/libedit/Makefile:104-122 ("Automatically generated file, do not
# edit" in each). Tracked libedit sources do not reference them by name.
/BSD/lib/libedit/vi.h
/BSD/lib/libedit/emacs.h
/BSD/lib/libedit/common.h
/BSD/lib/libedit/fcns.h
/BSD/lib/libedit/help.h
/BSD/lib/libedit/func.h

# launchd's mig output. BSD/sbin/launchd/Makefile lists these in CLEANFILES
# (line 59) and regenerates them from the tracked .def files; build_launchd.sh
# re-runs the same rules. Nothing hand-written lives under these names.
/BSD/sbin/launchd/*Server.c
/BSD/sbin/launchd/*User.c
/BSD/sbin/launchd/*Server.h
/BSD/sbin/launchd/job.h
/BSD/sbin/launchd/job_reply.h
/BSD/sbin/launchd/job_forward.h
/BSD/sbin/launchd/notify.h
/BSD/sbin/launchd/internal.h
/BSD/sbin/launchd/helper.h
/BSD/sbin/launchd/mach_exc.h

# An `ar` symbol table that escaped its archive and landed in the working
# directory. Build junk from a stray command run at the repo root.
/__.SYMDEF SORTED

Scratch from a standalone host compile, not part of any build system.
/t.c
/block.o
/lchflags.o
/llvm_target/
# In-tree build products from the Frameworks build. The Frameworks tree
# builds in place, so .o/.a/.dylib files land beside their sources.
/Frameworks/**/*.o
/Frameworks/**/*.a
/Frameworks/**/*.dylib
/Frameworks/**/*.so

# Frameworks_build is the CMake build output directory for the Frameworks.
/Frameworks_build/

# In-tree build products from the Libraries builds (MiscLibs, firehose, zlib).
/Libraries/MiscLibs/*.o
/Libraries/MiscLibs/*.a
/Libraries/libfirehose_kernel/*.o
/Libraries/zlib/*.o
/Libraries/zlib/*.a
